Found 2026-08-27 on the first live run against real Docker (29.7.2, API 1.55).

  sg docker -c 'API_DOCKER_TEST_HOST=unix:///var/run/docker.sock \
    API_DOCKER_TEST_WRITE=1 prove -lr t/'

t/secrets_configs.t does not skip, it DIES:

  503 "This node is not a swarm manager"

Docker serves /secrets and /configs only on a swarm manager. This daemon is
a plain single-node install with no swarm initialised, which is the normal
state for a development machine and the state most users will have.

Podman hides the problem: it serves /secrets from its own local secret
store with no swarm anywhere in sight, so the test file has never had to
consider the case. That difference is already recorded in the Changes entry
for the Secrets/Configs work -- what was missed is that the DOCKER side of
the same endpoint needs a gate.

The fix is a skip, not a swarm init: `docker swarm init` on a developer
machine is a real side effect on the host's networking, and no test should
do it. Gate on the 503, or probe /info for Swarm.LocalNodeState eq 'active'
before the live subtests run.

Note while fixing: the failure is a die, not a failed assertion, so it takes
the whole file down rather than one subtest. Check whether other files have
the same shape.

Related: the engine-detection helper live_engine() added to
Test::API::Docker::Mock in the same session is the natural place for a
can_swarm() sibling.
